  "worked_solution": "The position of the particle at $t=3$ is equal to its position at $t=0$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in [0,3].$ Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx(3)=x(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{3}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nCarrying out the computations, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(3) & =(e+1)+{\\int }_{0}^{3}({e}^{t/3}-1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =e+1+{[3{e}^{t/3}-t]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =e+1+([3{e}^{(3)/3}-(3)]-[3{e}^{(0)/3}-(0)]) \\\\  & =e+1+([3e-3]-[3]) \\\\  & =e+1+3e-6 \\\\  & =4e-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the position of the particle at $t=3$ is $x=4e-5.$",
